## At a glance

Where every part of @DISTRO_NAME@ comes from, then each package in @DISTRO_NAME@'s own apt repository. The versions are filled in by `make render` from each package's `packages/<dir>/debian/changelog.in`, so they are the versions this checkout builds. Linked words are defined under [Terms](#terms).

| Part | What | Source | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| Base | Ubuntu @BASE_VERSION@ (`@BASE_SERIES@`): the kernel (`linux-generic`), systemd, Mesa, Hyprland, the signed boot chain (`shim-signed`, `grub-efi-amd64-signed`), and every [package](#package) not named in the rows below. The build starts from mmdebstrap's `minbase` variant (the [Essential and Priority: required](#essential-and-priority) packages plus apt), and each Ubuntu package in the image is the version the archive had when the ISO's root filesystem was built (`make rootfs`), listed on the ISO in `casper/filesystem.manifest`; an installed system then updates from the archive | Ubuntu's [archive](#archive): `http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu` with the [suites](#suite-and-pocket) `@BASE_SERIES@` and `@BASE_SERIES@-updates`, and `http://security.ubuntu.com/ubuntu` with `@BASE_SERIES@-security`, all four [components](#component) (`scripts/rootfs-create.sh`). On an installed system: `/etc/apt/sources.list.d/ubuntu.sources` | @DISTRO_NAME@ is Ubuntu: Ubuntu's kernel, drivers, security updates and Secure Boot chain, unmodified |
| Package set | The packages an installed system has, chosen by name; nothing is installed and then removed | The [metapackages](#metapackage) `@DISTRO_ID@-base`, `@DISTRO_ID@-session` and `@DISTRO_ID@-desktop` (next table), assembled into a root filesystem by mmdebstrap with [Recommends](#recommends) on, as on a stock Ubuntu, and two packages pinned out (`scripts/rootfs-create.sh`; [Installing software](#installing-software-recommends-are-on-with-exceptions)) | Each package is there for a recorded reason (docs/3 to docs/7) |
| Added apt repository | @DISTRO_NAME@'s own: every package in the next table | <@APT_URL@>, suite `@BASE_SERIES@`, component `@APT_COMPONENT@`, signed with the key in `@DISTRO_ID@-archive-keyring`. On an installed system: `/etc/apt/sources.list.d/@DISTRO_ID@.sources` | What Ubuntu's archive lacks, has too old, or needs patched |
| Added apt repository | `chromium`, `chromium-common` | The xtradeb PPA (a personal package archive: an apt repository a Launchpad user publishes), `https://ppa.launchpadcontent.net/xtradeb/apps/ubuntu`, pinned at priority 400 (an apt preferences entry in `/etc/apt/preferences.d/xtradeb`; Ubuntu's archive has apt's default 500), so Ubuntu's archive wins for any package both carry. On an installed system: `/etc/apt/sources.list.d/xtradeb.sources` | Omarchy's browser; Ubuntu's archive has only placeholder packages for Chromium (docs/6, choice 3) |
| Added apt repository | `mise` | mise's own repository, `https://mise.jdx.dev/deb`, suite `stable`. On an installed system: `/etc/apt/sources.list.d/mise.sources` | Omarchy preinstalls mise, and Ubuntu's archive does not carry mise (docs/6, choice 69) |
| App stores | Nothing preinstalled from either store | Ubuntu's `snapd` (the Snap Store) and `flatpak` with Flathub as a system remote | Applications Ubuntu does not ship as packages install without setup (docs/6, choice 17) |
| Publishers' repositories | Chrome, VS Code, 1Password, Spotify, Signal, Tailscale and others; none in the image | Each publisher's own apt repository, added by the menu's Install entries when the user picks one | The publishers' own packages, as on Ubuntu |
| Omarchy port | Omarchy @OMARCHY_TAG@ on apt instead of pacman | The fork <@OMARCHY_FORK@>, branch `@OMARCHY_PORT_REF@` on top of the tag `@OMARCHY_TAG@`, built at commit `@OMARCHY_PORT_COMMIT@` into the `desktop` row of the next table; what the port leaves out, and why, is in the fork's [PORT-REMOVALS.md](@OMARCHY_FORK@/blob/@OMARCHY_PORT_REF@/PORT-REMOVALS.md) | Omarchy ships only for Arch Linux ([The Omarchy desktop](#the-omarchy-desktop)) |
| Installation ISO | The live session and the installer (Calamares), which installs onto an encrypted disk by default | Two squashfs layers (compressed read-only file system images, the second stacked on the first): the installed system's (the desktop root filesystem) and the live session's on top (casper, Ubuntu's boot code for live sessions, and Calamares's dependencies from Ubuntu's archive, plus `calamares`, `@DISTRO_ID@-live` and `@DISTRO_ID@-installer` from the next table); a package pool on the medium holding Ubuntu's NVIDIA driver packages (`restricted`), the file system tools and the hardware packages | An install works offline and removes nothing (docs/7, choices 20, 41, 55) |

The packages in @DISTRO_NAME@'s repository, one row per [source package](#source-package) (the directory under `packages/`). "This repository" means the package's files are all in its `packages/<dir>/`. A dash in the Patches column means none.

