## Terms

Each entry uses only terms defined above it. The first use of each term in the rest of this file links to its entry here.

### Package

A `.deb` file: one installable unit, such as `hyprland_0.56.2+ds-1_amd64.deb`. Inside the `.deb` file is an `ar` archive holding two tarballs, one with control metadata (name, version, architecture, the `Depends` list, maintainer scripts) and one with the files to unpack. `dpkg` installs packages; `apt` is the layer above dpkg that fetches packages and resolves their dependencies.

### Source package

What a package is built from: a `.dsc` description file plus a tarball of the source tree, which carries a `debian/` directory with `control` (names, dependencies, descriptions), `changelog` (the package version comes from this file), `rules` (a makefile, usually just `dh $@`), `copyright`, and small helper files such as `install`, `service` and `udev` that tell debhelper which files go where. `dpkg-buildpackage` turns a source package into one or more packages by running `debian/rules`. In this repo, each directory under `packages/` is a source package.

### Suite and pocket

A suite is a named set of packages published together. For Ubuntu a suite's name is the series codename: `@BASE_SERIES@` is the suite for Ubuntu @BASE_VERSION@. Pockets are suites that differ only by a suffix on that name: `@BASE_SERIES@` itself is the release pocket, the package set as it stood on release day and never changed afterwards; `@BASE_SERIES@-security` receives security fixes; `@BASE_SERIES@-updates` receives other fixes, with the security fixes copied in. Two more pockets exist: `-proposed`, where updates are staged for testing, and `-backports`, newer versions of selected packages. Until `@BASE_SERIES@` is released in October, the release pocket is still the moving development set and the other pockets are empty.

### Component

Each suite is split into four components by license and by who maintains the packages. `main` is free software that Canonical maintains and issues security updates for (the kernel, systemd, Mesa, apt). `restricted` is proprietary software Canonical still supports, mostly drivers and firmware, such as the NVIDIA packages. `universe` is free software maintained by the community on a best-effort basis; Hyprland, Quickshell, sbuild and aptly all live in `universe`. `multiverse` is community software with license restrictions, such as `nvtop` and the Intel media driver. This split decides whose security team covers each package.

### Archive

Not a tar file. An archive is an apt repository: a directory tree with a fixed layout, usually served over HTTP, that publishes suites, their components and their packages, with signed index files describing those packages. "The Ubuntu archive" is the archive at `archive.ubuntu.com`, and a mirror is a copy of the same tree at another URL. Here is what the Ubuntu archive looks like, fetched on 2026-10-01 and trimmed:

```
$ curl -s http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu/dists/@BASE_SERIES@/InRelease
-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----
Hash: SHA512
Origin: Ubuntu
Suite: @BASE_SERIES@
Date: Thu, 01 Oct 2026  4:24:42 UTC
Architectures: amd64 amd64v3 arm64 armhf i386 ppc64el riscv64 s390x
Components: main restricted universe multiverse
...then one line per index file, with its size and SHA256:
 709c71da...31008   1269941 main/binary-amd64/Packages.gz
 3929e8d4...4a979  18804355 universe/source/Sources.gz

$ curl -s .../dists/@BASE_SERIES@/universe/binary-amd64/Packages.gz | zcat | grep -A14 '^Package: hyprland$'
Package: hyprland
Version: 0.56.2+ds-1
Depends: libaquamarine13 (>= 0.14.0), libc6 (>= 2.43), libcairo2 (>= 1.2.4), ...
Filename: pool/universe/h/hyprland/hyprland_0.56.2+ds-1_amd64.deb
Size: 5257018

$ curl -sI .../pool/universe/h/hyprland/hyprland_0.56.2+ds-1_amd64.deb
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Length: 5257018
Content-Type: application/vnd.debian.binary-package
```

1. **A sources entry in apt's configuration is the entry point.** apt reads the list of archives to use from `/etc/apt/sources.list` (one line per archive, the older format) and from files in `/etc/apt/sources.list.d/` (`*.sources` files in the stanza format Ubuntu now uses). Both formats carry the same three facts: a base URL, a suite, and components. The one-line form `deb http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu @BASE_SERIES@ main universe` and this stanza mean the same thing:

   ```
   Types: deb
   URIs: http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu
   Suites: @BASE_SERIES@
   Components: main universe
   Signed-By: /usr/share/keyrings/ubuntu-archive-keyring.gpg
   ```

   Every other path below is derived from those three facts. In this project an apt sources entry appears in three places: `scripts/chroot-create.sh` passes three one-line entries to mmdebstrap on the command line, and mmdebstrap writes those entries into the chroot's `/etc/apt/sources.list`; an installed Ubuntu system has its own entries in `/etc/apt/sources.list.d/ubuntu.sources`; and `packages/archive-keyring/archive.sources` is the stanza for our archive, which the `@DISTRO_ID@-apt-source` package installs into `/etc/apt/sources.list.d/`.
2. **`dists/<suite>/InRelease` is the signed table of contents.** A GPG-signed text file with the suite's name, date, architectures and components, then one line per index file with its size and hash. The archive keyring is the key that verifies this signature. The older form of the same table of contents is a `Release` file plus a detached `Release.gpg` signature.
3. **`dists/<suite>/<component>/binary-<arch>/Packages.gz` is the index.** One stanza per package in that component: name, version, dependencies, size, hash, and a `Filename:` path into the pool. `source/Sources.gz` does the same for source packages.
4. **`pool/` holds the files.** The `.deb`, `.dsc` and tarballs sit at the paths the indexes name, grouped by component, first letter and source package.

An apt client never lists directories. apt downloads `InRelease`, checks its signature, downloads the index files whose hashes `InRelease` vouches for, then fetches exactly the pool paths the indexes name and checks each file's hash. Those steps are what `apt update` and then `apt install` do. Our own apt repository is an archive of the same shape: aptly writes `dists/@BASE_SERIES@/InRelease` signed with our key and a `pool/` directory beside `dists/`, and the S3 bucket only has to serve those paths as files. The transport doesn't matter: the publish test described in [docs/2 - build-infra.md](2%20-%20build-infra.md) read our tree over `file://`. Two package names follow from this layout: `@DISTRO_ID@-archive-keyring` holds the key that signs our archive, and `ubuntu-archive-keyring` holds the key that signs Ubuntu's archive.

### Essential and Priority

Two markers every package in an archive carries. `Essential: yes` marks the handful of packages dpkg refuses to remove: the shell, coreutils, dpkg itself, 21 packages in `@BASE_SERIES@`. `Priority` is one of `required`, `important`, `standard` or `optional`, a coarse ranking of how basic a package is. Tools that assemble a root filesystem choose their starting set from these two markers.

### Recommends

A package declares its relationships at three strengths. `Depends` must be installed. `Recommends` is for packages that would be found together with the declaring package in all but unusual installations, and apt installs Recommends by default (`APT::Install-Recommends "true"` is apt's stock setting). `Suggests` is never installed automatically. Recommends chains are how a small tool drags in half a desktop. In this project the build chroot has Recommends off (the reason is in the mmdebstrap section below); the distro image and an installed system have Recommends on, as a stock Ubuntu does, with the exceptions in [Installing software](#installing-software-recommends-are-on-with-exceptions).

### Metapackage

A package that contains no files and exists only for its dependency list, so that installing one name pulls in a whole set of packages. `build-essential` and `linux-generic` are metapackages, and so are `@DISTRO_ID@-base`, `@DISTRO_ID@-session` and `@DISTRO_ID@-desktop`.

### Build dependencies

The packages a source package needs installed to build, declared in its `Build-Depends` line. The one toolchain a package may assume without declaring it is `build-essential`, a metapackage that in `@BASE_SERIES@` depends on `libc6-dev` (the C library's headers), `gcc` and `g++` (version 14.2 or newer), `make`, and `dpkg-dev` (the tools that run `debian/rules` and produce `.deb` and `.dsc` files). Everything else, from `debhelper` to any library's `-dev` package, must be named in `Build-Depends`.

### Chroot

A directory holding a complete root filesystem, used by running a program with that directory as its `/`. A build chroot contains only the essential packages, the toolchain and apt; a build inside a build chroot can only use what its source package declared, which is how a successful build proves a `Build-Depends` line is complete. This project keeps its build chroot as a tarball; sbuild unpacks a fresh copy of that tarball for every build and deletes the copy when the build ends.

### Vendored crates

A Rust program's crates (its library dependencies, which cargo normally downloads from crates.io) copied into the [source package](#source-package), so that `make build` (which `make release` and CI run) can compile the program inside the build [chroot](#chroot), where sbuild's unshare mode (`sbuild.conf`) blocks network access. Nothing is compiled when a user installs @DISTRO_NAME@: the ISO and the apt repository carry the finished packages. For a package whose `upstream` file says `CARGO_VENDOR=1` (hyprland-preview-share-picker, ttfx, asusctl, supergfxctl, qmk-hid), `scripts/build.sh` runs `cargo vendor --locked vendor` on the workstation: cargo downloads every crate at the version in the program's `Cargo.lock` into a `vendor/` directory, and `scripts/build.sh` packs that directory as a second tarball of the source package, such as `ttfx_0.5.0.orig-vendor.tar.gz`. In the build, the package's `debian/rules` points cargo at `vendor/` instead of crates.io. Ubuntu's own Rust packages instead depend on one `librust-*-dev` package per crate from the archive. Because the crates' code ships inside the source package, `scripts/vendor-copyright.py` lists each crate's license in `debian/copyright`.

### Codec

The code that turns one compressed audio, video or image format (H.264, AAC, AV1, HEIC) into pictures and sound, or the reverse. On Linux a program gets its codecs from one of two libraries: FFmpeg (`libavcodec`), which mpv, `ffmpeg` and `ffmpegthumbnailer` use, or GStreamer, which GNOME's players use. GStreamer's codecs come as plugins, which the GStreamer project sorts into sets by quality and licensing, one Ubuntu package per set. The sets' meanings below are quoted from the packages' descriptions in Ubuntu's `@BASE_SERIES@` archive (read 2026-10-19):

| Set | Ubuntu package | Package description |
|---|---|---|
| `base` | `gstreamer1.0-plugins-base` | "an essential exemplary set of elements" |
| `good` | `gstreamer1.0-plugins-good` | "a set of good-quality plug-ins under the LGPL license" |
| `ugly` | `gstreamer1.0-plugins-ugly` | "a set of good-quality plug-ins that might pose distribution problems" (patents or licenses; the H.264 encoder `x264` and DVD reading are here) |
| `bad` | `gstreamer1.0-plugins-bad` | "plug-ins that aren't up to par compared to the rest. They might be close to being good quality, but they're missing something - be it a good code review, some documentation, a set of tests, a real live maintainer, or some actual wide use" (the OpenH264 decoder is here) |
| `libav` | `gstreamer1.0-libav` | "supports a large number of audio and video compression formats through the use of the ffmpeg library": GStreamer's wrapper around FFmpeg, which gives GStreamer players FFmpeg's H.264, HEVC and AAC decoders |

The packages that ship the x264 plugin (`libgstx264.so`), DVD reading (`libgstdvdread.so`) and OpenH264 (`libgstopenh264.so`) are from the archive's `Contents-amd64` index. `base` and `good` are in Ubuntu's main component; `ugly`, `bad` and `libav` are in universe.

### VA-API driver

The library through which a program hands [codec](#codec) work to the graphics card instead of the processor (hardware decoding). VA-API is the interface; each GPU vendor has its own driver: Mesa's for AMD, Intel's `intel-media-va-driver` for Intel, and `nvidia-vaapi-driver` for NVIDIA.

## To do

The one list of open work for @DISTRO_NAME@, this repository and the fork, is the GitHub project [Oaxaca](https://github.com/users/santisbon/projects/4): one draft issue per item, each with its Area (such as "To design" or "When something changes upstream") and its Status. Each item says what is known and where its background is. When an item is done, it is marked Done there, and the work is recorded where the work happened: a changelog entry, a numbered choice in one of the step documents, or the fork's [PORT-REMOVALS.md](@OMARCHY_FORK@/blob/@OMARCHY_PORT_REF@/PORT-REMOVALS.md), "Restored".

## The installed system in detail

What an installed @DISTRO_NAME@ system does differently from a stock Ubuntu, in more detail than the [top-level README](../README.md), which is the introduction for people deciding whether to install @DISTRO_NAME@. Each part names the files and commands involved.

### Installing software: Recommends are on, with exceptions

apt on @DISTRO_NAME@ installs a package's required dependencies (`Depends`) and the packages it recommends ([Recommends](#recommends)), as on a stock Ubuntu, with the exceptions below. Two packages are never installed: `@DISTRO_ID@-base` ships apt pins in `/etc/apt/preferences.d/@DISTRO_ID@-blocked-recommends` (an apt preferences file: one stanza per package, `Pin-Priority: -1` meaning "never install") that keep out:

- `xdg-desktop-portal-gnome`: Files recommends it, and it recommends `gnome-shell`, which would bring GNOME Shell, `gdm3` (a second login screen next to SDDM), the Ubuntu GNOME session and IBus.
- `sudo-rs`: `sudo` recommends it, and it would take over the `sudo` command from classic sudo, whose sudoers rules @DISTRO_NAME@'s menus use.

To allow one of them, delete its stanza from that file (dpkg asks about the edit when a new `@DISTRO_ID@-base` changes the file).

The desktop's own package command installs without Recommends: `@DISTRO_ID@-pkg-add` runs `apt-get install --no-install-recommends`, because its callers name every package they need. The menu's Install entries call `@DISTRO_ID@-pkg-add` (29 of the port's commands do), and so do plugins that use Omarchy's `omarchy-pkg-add`; `@DISTRO_ID@-reinstall-pkgs` does the same. Install > Package (`@DISTRO_ID@-pkg-install`, a picker over the whole archive) and the entries that install a publisher's downloaded `.deb` (Chrome, Minecraft, Docker Desktop) keep apt's default and install Recommends.

To install a single package without its Recommends:

```sh
sudo apt install --no-install-recommends <package>
```

@DISTRO_NAME@ systems installed before `@DISTRO_ID@-base` 0.1.6 were built with Recommends off. Updating removes that setting, but apt does not go back and add the Recommends it skipped; this command does, once (`--solver internal` selects apt's classic solver: with apt 3.3's default solver, `--fix-policy` added nothing when tried on 2026-10-14):

```sh
sudo apt-get --solver internal --fix-policy install
```

### Secure Boot

@DISTRO_NAME@ boots with Secure Boot on or off and never asks the user to enroll a key. Both the live USB and the installed system boot through Ubuntu's own signed chain: shim (signed by Microsoft, so the firmware trusts it) loads Canonical's signed GRUB and kernel. Secure Boot is supported, never required: @DISTRO_NAME@ does not check or change the firmware setting.

### NVIDIA and AMD GPUs

The installer detects an NVIDIA GPU and installs NVIDIA's open driver from the newest branch that has Canonical's prebuilt, signed kernel modules. Because Canonical signed the modules, the driver loads under Secure Boot without a key of your own, and because the driver packages ship on the USB image, the installation needs no network. AMD GPUs need nothing installed: they run on the `amdgpu` driver in Ubuntu's signed kernel and on Mesa, both already in the system.

On laptops with hybrid graphics, where the built-in screen hangs off the Intel or AMD GPU and a discrete NVIDIA or AMD GPU sits beside it, the desktop renders on the display GPU and applications default to it, so the discrete GPU is left alone until something asks for it:

- the app menu starts a program on the discrete GPU when its desktop entry asks for one (Steam's does), or when its id is listed in `~/.config/@DISTRO_ID@/dgpu-apps` (a line `-<id>` keeps a program on the display GPU);
- `@DISTRO_ID@-launch-dgpu <command>` runs one program on the discrete GPU (in Steam: `@DISTRO_ID@-launch-dgpu %command%`): through NVIDIA's PRIME render offload on NVIDIA, through Mesa's `DRI_PRIME` on AMD;
- CUDA programs use an NVIDIA GPU directly, with no launcher.

The AMD path is tested against simulated machines only, not yet on AMD hardware.

### Media codecs

Which [codecs](#codec) and [VA-API drivers](#va-api-driver) a stock Ubuntu Desktop, Omarchy and @DISTRO_NAME@ install, checked on 2026-10-19:

- the Ubuntu Desktop column: the `ubuntu-desktop` metapackage 1.577 and `ubuntu-restricted-addons` 33ubuntu in Ubuntu's `@BASE_SERIES@` archive, and the archive's `Contents-amd64` index for which package ships each GStreamer plugin;
- the Omarchy column: Omarchy v4.0.6's `install/omarchy-base.packages` and hardware scripts, and Arch's packages on archlinux.org (Omarchy installs Arch's current versions, such as `ffmpeg` 9.0.2-2);
- the @DISTRO_NAME@ column: the root filesystem manifest of the `desktop` 4.0.4+git20261019.108.aa6bd56b release, and the `@DISTRO_ID@-session` package's dependencies.

| | Ubuntu Desktop @BASE_VERSION@ | Omarchy v4.0.6 | @DISTRO_NAME@ |
|---|---|---|---|
| Video player | Showtime (GStreamer) | mpv (FFmpeg) | mpv (FFmpeg) |
| Music player | Rhythmbox (GStreamer) | none; mpv plays music files | none; mpv plays music files |
| FFmpeg | not named by `ubuntu-desktop` | Arch's `ffmpeg`, a dependency of `mpv` | Ubuntu's `ffmpeg` 8.1.2 (`libavcodec62`) |
| GStreamer plugins | `base` (through `gstreamer1.0-plugins-base-apps`) | none named | `base` and `good` 1.28.7 |
| GStreamer `bad`, `ugly` and `libav` | only in `ubuntu-restricted-addons`, which the installer's "Install third-party software … additional media formats" box installs | not installed | not installed |
| H.264, HEVC and AAC in the video player | only with `ubuntu-restricted-addons` (`gstreamer1.0-libav`) | yes: FFmpeg's own decoders | yes: FFmpeg's own decoders |
| AV1 in the video player | only with `ubuntu-restricted-addons` (`gstreamer1.0-libav`) | yes: Arch's `ffmpeg` depends on `dav1d` | yes: `libdav1d7` |
| H.264 and HEVC encoders (`@DISTRO_ID@-transcode`, Omarchy's `omarchy-transcode`) | not installed | Arch's `ffmpeg` depends on `x264` and `x265` | `libx264-165` and `libx265-216` |
| HEIC photos | only with `ubuntu-restricted-addons` (`libheif-plugin-libde265`) | yes: `imv` depends on Arch's `libheif`, which depends on `libde265` | not in the `.108` image, where `libheif1` had only the AVIF plugins (`libheif-plugin-aomdec`, `-aomenc`); yes from the next `desktop` release on, which depends on `libheif-plugin-libde265` (the port's `install/@DISTRO_ID@-base.packages`), and the boot test decodes a sample HEIC |
| DVD reading (no CSS decryption) | not named by `ubuntu-desktop` | `libdvdread` and `libdvdnav`, dependencies of Arch's `ffmpeg` and `mpv` | `libdvdread8t64` and `libdvdnav4` |
| Hardware decoding, AMD | Mesa's VA-API driver in `mesa-libgallium`, which `Provides: mesa-va-drivers` | Mesa's VA-API driver in Arch's `mesa` (`Provides: libva-mesa-driver`) | `mesa-libgallium` 26.2.4 (`radeonsi_drv_video.so`) |
| Hardware decoding, Intel | not named by `ubuntu-desktop` | `intel-media-driver`, on Intel machines (`install/hardware/intel/video-acceleration.sh`) | `intel-media-va-driver-non-free` 26.2.4, on every machine (a dependency of `@DISTRO_ID@-session`) |
| Hardware decoding, NVIDIA | not named by `ubuntu-desktop` | `libva-nvidia-driver`, on NVIDIA machines (`install/hardware/nvidia.sh`) | `nvidia-vaapi-driver`, which `@DISTRO_ID@-drivers-install` adds after `ubuntu-drivers` installs an NVIDIA driver ([NVIDIA and AMD GPUs](#nvidia-and-amd-gpus)) |
| Browser | Firefox (snap); which web video formats the snap plays is not checked | Arch's Chromium, built with `proprietary_codecs=true` (Arch's `PKGBUILD`, line 350), so web pages can play H.264 and AAC | Chromium 155 from xtradeb; whether xtradeb's build plays H.264 and AAC on web pages is not checked |

"Not named by `ubuntu-desktop`" means the metapackage neither depends on nor recommends the package; whether another package of a stock install pulls the package in is not checked. None of @DISTRO_NAME@'s preinstalled programs plays media through GStreamer, so the missing `bad`, `ugly` and `libav` plugins matter only for a GStreamer player the user adds, such as Showtime; Omarchy installs none of them either.

### Apple hardware

The [top-level README](../README.md) lists what @DISTRO_NAME@ does on Apple hardware; this section gives each item's mechanism and the file it comes from. Only the Studio Display has been tried, on the Legion laptop; no Mac has run @DISTRO_NAME@ yet.

How the Mac fixes are applied: the installer's system step runs `@DISTRO_ID@-apply-system`, which runs `@DISTRO_ID@-apply-hardware`, which runs the Omarchy fork's `install/hardware/all.sh`, and that script runs one script per hardware fix. Each script reads the machine's model name (`/sys/class/dmi/id/product_name`, such as `MacBook8,1`) or its PCI IDs (the vendor and device numbers each chip reports, listed by `lspci -nn`, such as `14e4:4331`), and changes nothing on other machines. Every path below that starts with `install/` or `bin/` is in the fork (<@OMARCHY_FORK@>, branch `@OMARCHY_PORT_REF@`).

#### Apple Studio Display

- **Brightness.** The Studio Display takes brightness commands over its USB connection, as USB HID reports through the kernel's `hiddev` devices; Linux has no standard brightness control for an external display connected this way. `asdcontrol` sends those commands (`packages/asdcontrol`, built from `omakasui/asdcontrol`; Ubuntu's archive does not carry it). The brightness keys and the bar's brightness control run `@DISTRO_ID@-brightness-display-apple` (`bin/omarchy-brightness-display-apple`), which runs `asdcontrol` through sudo, because opening a `hiddev` device needs root. The asdcontrol package's sudoers rule, `/etc/sudoers.d/50-asdcontrol` (from `packages/asdcontrol/debian/asdcontrol.sudoers`), lets members of the `sudo` group run `asdcontrol` without a password, and only with `hiddev` device paths and a brightness value as arguments.
- **Thunderbolt.** The display connects over Thunderbolt, and Ubuntu's `bolt` authorizes the connection.
- **Dark display after boot.** `@DISTRO_ID@-base` 0.1.8 ships `/usr/lib/modprobe.d/@DISTRO_ID@-thunderbolt.conf` with `options thunderbolt dprx_timeout=-1`, so the kernel's Thunderbolt driver waits for the GPU driver without a time limit before giving up on the display's DisplayPort connection; without the option, the Studio Display sometimes stayed dark until its cable was replugged ([docs/7](7%20-%20image-installer.md), choice 49).
- **Speakers, microphones and camera.** No @DISTRO_NAME@ package or setting is specific to them, and no test checks them on their own.

#### MacBooks from 2012 and early 2013: the BCM4331 Wi-Fi

`install/hardware/fix-bcm43xx.sh`, on PCI ID `14e4:4331`, installs Ubuntu's `firmware-b43-installer`. The kernel's `b43` driver runs the chip but needs Broadcom's firmware, which may not be redistributed, so the package downloads Broadcom's driver during installation and extracts the firmware from it: installing on these MacBooks needs a network connection. Omarchy installs `broadcom-wl-dkms` for this chip instead; the port changed the script because of the Secure Boot reason under "Not supported" below.

#### Macs from 2015 on with Broadcom Wi-Fi: the WPA handshake

`brcmfmac` is the kernel's driver for Broadcom's newer Wi-Fi chips, whose firmware can perform the WPA four-way handshake (the exchange that checks the network password) itself. On Apple's parts that offload fails against an access point in WPA2/WPA3 transition mode (one that accepts both): the handshake never completes, and NetworkManager reports the password as wrong. `install/hardware/apple/fix-brcmfmac-supplicant.sh` (Omarchy's script, unchanged) writes `/etc/modprobe.d/brcmfmac.conf` with `options brcmfmac feature_disable=0x82000`, which turns off the firmware supplicant (`0x2000`) and authenticator (`0x80000`), so `wpa_supplicant` performs the handshake in software. The script acts on a Mac with the T2 bridge (PCI IDs `106b:1801` or `106b:1802`), or on a machine whose vendor is Apple with one of brcmfmac's chip IDs `14e4:43ba`, `43bb`, `43bc`, `43a3`, `43dc`, `4464`, `4488`, `4425` or `4433`: the BCM43602 of 2015 to 2017 Macs, the BCM4350, BCM4355 and BCM4364 of 2018 and 2019 Macs (the iMac19,1 and iMac19,2 have no T2 chip), and the BCM4377, BCM4378 and BCM4387 of the T2 Macs.

#### The 12-inch MacBook and the 2016 and 2017 MacBook Pros: the keyboard at the encryption prompt

These models' keyboards connect over SPI (a chip-to-chip bus) instead of USB, through the kernel's `applespi` driver and the `spi_pxa2xx_platform` and `intel_lpss_pci` modules. The disk encryption passphrase prompt runs from the initramfs (the small file system the kernel starts from, before the encrypted root is unlocked), so those modules have to be in the initramfs. Omarchy installs `macbook12-spi-driver-dkms` and lists the modules in a mkinitcpio drop-in. Ubuntu's kernel already has the driver as signed modules, and Ubuntu's initramfs (built by initramfs-tools with `MODULES=most`) already carries the three modules (read from the image's initramfs on 2026-10-16). The one module on Omarchy's lists that Ubuntu's initramfs lacks is `spi_pxa2xx_pci`, which Omarchy adds for the `MacBook8,1` only, so `install/hardware/apple/fix-spi-keyboard.sh` puts a hook in `/etc/initramfs-tools/hooks/` on that model that adds the module and loads it at boot.

#### The same models: the SSD waking from sleep

`install/hardware/apple/fix-suspend-nvme.sh` (Omarchy's script, unchanged) acts on the `MacBook8,1`, `MacBook9,1` and `MacBook10,1` (the 12-inch MacBooks) and the `MacBookPro13,1` to `13,3` and `MacBookPro14,1` to `14,3` (the 2016 and 2017 MacBook Pros). When the SSD sits at PCI address `0000:01:00.0`, the script enables a systemd unit that writes `0` to the SSD's `d3cold_allowed` at every boot, which keeps the SSD out of D3cold, the deepest PCI power state, from which these drives fail to wake.

#### Not supported

- **Macs with Apple's T2 chip** (most Macs from 2018 to 2020). Omarchy's `install/hardware/apple/fix-t2.sh` sets these Macs up with an out-of-tree kernel and that kernel's pacman repository; the port removed the script (the fork's `PORT-REMOVALS.md`, "Apple T2").
- **The Broadcom BCM4360 Wi-Fi** of 2013 to 2015 MacBooks. The chip's only driver is Broadcom's out-of-tree `wl`, packaged as `broadcom-sta-dkms`: DKMS builds the module on the machine, and with Secure Boot on the kernel loads such a module only when it is signed with a Machine Owner Key, which @DISTRO_NAME@ never asks the user to enroll (`PORT-REMOVALS.md`, "The BCM4360's Wi-Fi driver").
- **Macs with Apple silicon** (M1 and later). @DISTRO_NAME@ is built only for 64-bit Intel and AMD processors (amd64).

### The Omarchy desktop

[Omarchy](https://github.com/omacom/omarchy) is a Linux distribution: Arch Linux's base (kernel, pacman, boot loader, snapshots) with Omarchy's own layer on top, shipped as two Arch packages (the commands, the Quickshell shell, the themes, and the settings, units and starter files a new user gets) plus a package list and setup scripts that tune the Arch base. @DISTRO_NAME@ takes only that layer and puts Ubuntu underneath instead of Arch. In @DISTRO_NAME@'s fork of Omarchy (the "Omarchy port" row of [At a glance](#at-a-glance), which names the fork, branch and commit), pacman and the AUR are replaced by apt (and, for the few applications Ubuntu does not ship as regular packages, the Snap Store or Flathub), the scripts that configured Arch's base are dropped in favor of Ubuntu's own configuration, and the package list is translated to Ubuntu's names. The build turns the fork into the five packages of the `desktop` row, and updates arrive through apt like the rest of the system.

### Plugins and themes written for Omarchy

@DISTRO_NAME@ runs the plugins in Omarchy's plugin marketplace (<https://plugins.omarchy.org>) and the themes on Omarchy's theme list (<https://omarchy.org/themes/>). A plugin is a git repository of code that runs inside the desktop's shell (the bar, its panels, the menu); a theme is a git repository of colors, wallpapers and per-application settings. Add a plugin with Menu > Setup > Plugins > Add Plugin (or `@DISTRO_ID@ plugin add <git URL>`), and a theme with Menu > Install > Style > Theme (or `@DISTRO_ID@ theme install <git URL>`).

Plugins and themes are written for Omarchy, so they call Omarchy's names. @DISTRO_NAME@ answers to those names through a compatibility layer, while everything you see says @DISTRO_NAME@:

- every `@DISTRO_ID@-<name>` command also answers to `omarchy-<name>`, the `@DISTRO_ID@` command to `omarchy`, and `/usr/share/@DISTRO_ID@` to `/usr/share/omarchy`; your `~/.config/omarchy`, `~/.local/state/omarchy` and `~/.local/share/omarchy` are links to @DISTRO_NAME@'s folders. These are links: the commands the menu and the key bindings run are the `@DISTRO_ID@-*` ones, and the `@DISTRO_ID@` command introduces itself as @DISTRO_NAME@'s even when called as `omarchy`;
- names you do not see keep Omarchy's spelling: the built-in plugins' IDs (`omarchy.clock`, `omarchy.weather`), `OMARCHY_*` environment variables, and app IDs such as `org.omarchy.terminal`.

Omarchy runs on Arch Linux and @DISTRO_NAME@ on Ubuntu, so a plugin or theme that installs software the Arch way needs more than names. Three things handle that:

- **A warning when you add one.** Before keeping a plugin or theme, @DISTRO_NAME@ looks through its files for Arch's package tools (`pacman`, `yay`, `paru`, `makepkg`, Omarchy's AUR commands) and pacman's files, shows up to 10 lines where they appear, and asks "Add it anyway?" with No as the default. A match is a hint: the words also appear in comments and messages, and such a plugin may work fine.
- **Stand-ins for Arch's package tools.** `/usr/lib/@DISTRO_ID@/arch-standins/` holds `pacman`, `yay`, `paru` and `makepkg`, all running `@DISTRO_ID@-arch-standin`, and that folder comes last on your `PATH`, so a real program of the same name wins if you install one (Ubuntu's `pacman-package-manager` has a real `pacman`). A query such as `pacman -Q zip` answers "not installed" without a word, so a plugin that only checks takes its "missing" path. Anything else, such as `yay -S xray`, changes nothing: it prints the command that ran, says that @DISTRO_NAME@ installs software with apt, shows a desktop notification, and exits with status 1. Omarchy's AUR commands (`omarchy-pkg-aur-add`, `-install`, `-accessible`) do the same, and the last one answers that the AUR is not available.
- **Package-name translations.** Omarchy's own package command, `omarchy-pkg-add <names>` (here `@DISTRO_ID@-pkg-add`), installs with apt on @DISTRO_NAME@, and so do the checks `omarchy-pkg-present` and `omarchy-pkg-missing`. When a plugin gives them an Arch package name that Ubuntu does not use, they look it up in a translation table and use the Ubuntu packages listed there; `python-<name>` becomes `python3-<name>` when Ubuntu has that; any other name is used as it is.

The translation table is a plain text file, one Arch name per line followed by its Ubuntu packages, lines starting with `#` ignored:

- on an installed system: `/usr/share/@DISTRO_ID@/default/@DISTRO_ID@/arch-package-names`;
- in the source: `default/omarchy/arch-package-names` in the Omarchy fork (<@OMARCHY_FORK@>, branch `@OMARCHY_PORT_REF@`).

Lines from that file:

```
bluez-obex bluez-obexd
github-cli gh
qt6-declarative qml6-module-qtquick qml6-module-qtquick-controls qml6-module-qtquick-layouts qml6-module-qtquick-window qml6-module-qtqml-workerscript qml6-module-qtquick-templates
ttf-jetbrains-mono-nerd @DISTRO_ID@-fonts
```

The table has 162 entries (2026-10-17), drawn from every package name that the 5602 plugins listed in Omarchy's marketplace ask for in their code, build files and messages (5575 of them could be fetched; 881 name a package), plus the Arch packages Omarchy itself installs. Each Ubuntu package was chosen by the files both distributions install (commands, libraries, Python modules), checked by hand, and is in Ubuntu @BASE_VERSION@'s archive or @DISTRO_NAME@'s own repository. An Arch name that is also an Ubuntu package name is never in the table, so the table cannot change what an Ubuntu name installs. Names with no entry are installed as they are, and apt says so when Ubuntu has no such package: software Ubuntu does not package (such as `ruff`, `croc`, `himalaya` or `spotifyd`), AUR-only packages, and the publishers' own packages (Chrome, VS Code, 1Password, Spotify, Signal, Tailscale, Mullvad), which come from the publishers' repositories through the menu's Install > Browser, Editor and Service entries instead. To add a translation, edit the file in the fork and open a pull request against `@OMARCHY_PORT_REF@`; on your own machine you can also edit the installed copy, which the next update of `@DISTRO_ID@-config` replaces.

What no layer reaches: plugins that need software Ubuntu does not package (for example `xray`, which is only in Arch's user repository, the AUR), features built on pacman's own files (a widget that watches pacman's log stays idle), and commands Omarchy added after the version @DISTRO_NAME@ carries (@OMARCHY_TAG@). The reasoning, and the sample of marketplace plugins it was measured on, is choice 86 in [docs/6 - omarchy-port.md](6%20-%20omarchy-port.md).

## Repository map

```
distro.conf          the only place the distro's name and identity live
Makefile             entry points: render, branding, check-versions, upstream-check, chroot, build, publish, iso-publish, rootfs, rootfs-test, drivers-check, iso, iso-test, upgrade-test, session-upgrade-test, factory-reset-test, prune
sbuild.conf          sbuild settings for this repo (loaded through SBUILD_CONFIG)
scripts/
  render.py          fills @@TOKEN@@ templates (*.in) from distro.conf
  branding.py        draws the distro's artwork (the mark, the wordmark, the text art, the installer's images) into the packages (make branding; docs/6, choice 104)
  keygen.sh          creates the apt signing key (run once, by hand)
  chroot-create.sh   builds the clean build chroot with mmdebstrap
  build.sh           builds packages with sbuild, checks them with lintian
  machine-setup.sh   installs the tools, checks the credentials, builds the chroot and seeds aptly, once per workstation (make machine-setup)
  release.sh         builds what is unpublished, tests the image, commits and publishes (make release); prints the release commit's message (make release-message)
  iso-infra.sh       creates or updates the ISO downloads' bucket, CloudFront distribution, certificate and DNS records at @ISO_HOST@ (make iso-infra; infra/iso/)
  budget-infra.sh    creates or updates the AWS account's monthly cost budget of $@BUDGET_LIMIT_USD@ and its email alerts (make budget-infra)
  iso-publish.sh     uploads build/iso's ISO with its signed SHA256SUMS and latest.json to @ISO_HOST@, keeping the newest @ISO_KEEP@ (make iso-publish; asked by make release)
  build-prune.sh     deletes every build in build/ but the newest of each package (make prune; run by make release)
  repo-publish.sh    publishes build/ into the apt repository with aptly; refuses a published version with different content; --seed fills a new workstation's aptly database from the published repository
  version-check.sh   fails when a package directory changed since the last v* tag without a version bump (step 8)
  upgrade-test.sh    upgrades the published packages to build/ in a throwaway chroot and runs the migrations (step 8)
  upstream-check.sh  fails when a program repackaged from its GitHub release (RELEASES= in packages/*/upstream) has a newer release (make upstream-check)
  vendor-copyright.py  writes the debian/copyright paragraphs for a vendored Rust crate tree, from each crate's license field (texts in licenses/)
  rootfs-create.sh   builds the root filesystem tarball with mmdebstrap (steps 3, 4, 6 and 7; PROFILE=base|session|desktop|live)
  keys/xtradeb.gpg   the xtradeb PPA's signing key, for the Chromium the desktop and live profiles install (docs/6, choice 3)
  rootfs-boot-test.sh  boots that tarball in QEMU and checks the base system, the graphical session and, for desktop, the ported desktop;
                       UPGRADE=1 updates the published desktop to build/ inside its running session first
  boot-test-bindings.lua, boot-test-bindings.py
                       the boot test's keybinding lister (Lua) and the check of the programs the bindings run
  drivers-check.sh   runs ubuntu-drivers inside the tarball against this machine's GPU (step 4)
  iso-create.sh      turns the live tarball into the installation ISO: squashfs, signed shim and GRUB in an EFI image, xorriso (step 7)
  iso-boot-test.sh   boots the test ISO in QEMU under OVMF, Secure Boot on or off, and checks the live session and the installer
  factory-reset-test.sh  installs the test ISO on an encrypted btrfs disk in QEMU, factory-resets it, and checks the new owner's machine
  suspend-baseline.sh  one measured suspend/resume cycle, used for the baseline
packages/<dir>/       one source package per directory: debian/, and an "upstream" file naming what a
                     third-party package is built from; each one is a row of "At a glance"
.github/workflows/build.yml   CI: the same make targets on an Ubuntu runner
.github/workflows/upstream-check.yml   CI, weekly: make upstream-check
infra/apt-repo/      the apt repository's hosting: its CloudFormation template and README (make apt-infra)
infra/iso/           the ISO downloads' hosting at @ISO_HOST@: its CloudFormation template and README (make iso-infra)
infra/budget/        the AWS account's monthly cost budget and its email alerts: its CloudFormation template and README (make budget-infra)
docs/                the developer README (this file), plan, policy, build infrastructure, root filesystem, session layer, Hyprland and Quickshell, the Omarchy port, image and installer, update path, suspend baseline
tests/
  factory-reset/       the guest's side of make factory-reset-test: the scripted install, the reset, the checks, a stand-in for gum
  iso-drive/<name>/    driven installer and live-session checks: DRIVE=tests/iso-drive/<name> make iso-test puts the folder's run.sh on a disk the guest runs (docs/7, choices 26, 38, 41, 42)
  gpu-env/run.sh       the session's GPU script against made-up machines (docs/7, choice 43); runs directly, no build
  launch-dgpu/run.sh   the port's menu launcher and discrete-GPU launcher, from a fork checkout (docs/7, choice 43); runs directly
build/               build output (not committed)
```

## The tools

Each `make` target runs one script, and each script drives one program. The program is the thing worth understanding; the script only pins that program's options.

### `distro.conf` and `scripts/render.py` (`make render`)

The distro's name, `@DISTRO_NAME@`, and its lowercase identifier, `@DISTRO_ID@`, appear in package names, commands, paths, systemd units, the apt `Origin` field and the signing key's identity. Every one of those occurrences is generated. `distro.conf` is a shell-syntax file with `DISTRO_ID=@DISTRO_ID@`, `DISTRO_NAME=@DISTRO_NAME@`, the base series, the GitHub names and the apt settings. Any file that needs one of those values is a template ending in `.in`, with tokens such as `@@DISTRO_ID@@` (doubling the at-signs yields a literal token, as this sentence does). `make render` writes the real file next to each template and stamps the rendered file with a "generated, edit the .in" header. Rendered files are committed so a clone is complete, and CI fails if the rendered files drift from `distro.conf`. A rebrand is an edit to `distro.conf` plus the rebrand steps in [docs/2 - build-infra.md](2%20-%20build-infra.md).

```
make render            # render every *.in
make check-render      # what CI runs: render, then fail if anything changed
```

### `mmdebstrap` (`make chroot`)

mmdebstrap builds a Debian or Ubuntu root filesystem straight from an archive, without root privileges, into a directory or a tarball. Give it a [suite](#suite-and-pocket) name, an output path (an empty directory to fill, or a `.tar` file to write; mmdebstrap's manual calls this the target) and one or more apt sources entries, and mmdebstrap downloads the chosen package set, unpacks and configures those packages, and writes the resulting root filesystem to that path. This project uses mmdebstrap twice. Today, `make chroot` uses it to make the clean build environment, saved as `~/.cache/sbuild/@BASE_SERIES@-amd64.tar` (about 430 MB, 118 packages). Later, in roadmap step 3, the same tool builds the distro's own root filesystem. The command in `scripts/chroot-create.sh` is, in words: the `buildd` variant of `@BASE_SERIES@`, from the `main` and `universe` [components](#component), with the `-updates` and `-security` [pockets](#suite-and-pocket), [Recommends](#recommends) off. The variants are explained below; the reasons for the other three options follow here. `main` and `universe` are where everything we build against lives, while the distro image additionally enables `restricted` and `multiverse`, as [docs/0 - plan.md](0%20-%20plan.md) explains. All three pockets are enabled because a [chroot](#chroot) built from the release pocket alone would build against package versions that users no longer run. Recommends are off so that a build only sees what its package declares: if a build quietly needed something a Recommends line happened to pull in, the build would pass in the chroot and fail on any system where that package is absent. The distro image does not share this setting: since 2026-10-14 the image installs Recommends, as a stock Ubuntu does, and keeps two packages out with apt pins ([Installing software](#installing-software-recommends-are-on-with-exceptions); [docs/3](3%20-%20rootfs.md), choice 4).

```
make chroot                          # creates the chroot tarball, or keeps the existing one if under a week old
scripts/chroot-create.sh --force     # rebuild the tarball now
UBUNTU_MIRROR=http://... make chroot  # use a closer mirror
```

**Variants.** `--variant` picks the starting package set from the [Essential and Priority](#essential-and-priority) markers. The variants build on each other:

| Variant | Package set | Used for |
|---|---|---|
| `essential` | only `Essential: yes` packages | the floor the other variants stand on |
| `apt` | essential plus apt | |
| `minbase` | essential plus every `Priority: required` package, which includes apt | the distro's own root filesystem in step 3: enough to run apt, with nothing that presumes a use |
| `buildd` | essential plus apt plus `build-essential` and its dependency closure | this build chroot; `buildd` mirrors what Debian's and Ubuntu's build machines, the "buildds", start from |
| `important`, `standard` | minbase plus `Priority: important`, then `standard` too | not used. `important` is debootstrap's default and brings editors, network tools and documentation that nobody asked for |

So `minbase` and `buildd` are siblings, not a ladder: `minbase` is "enough to run and install things", `buildd` is "enough to compile things", and each lacks the other's extras. The build chroot has no desktop, no kernel and no init. Each package's declared [build dependencies](#build-dependencies) are added by sbuild at build time, into a throwaway copy of the chroot, as the next paragraph describes.

**Why `buildd` and nothing more.** The chroot tarball holds the essential set, apt and `build-essential`'s dependency closure, 118 packages in all. For every build, sbuild unpacks a fresh copy of that tarball, installs the package's declared build dependencies into the copy, builds there, and deletes the copy afterwards. The tarball itself never changes, and a build can only use what its package declared, which is why a successful build in the chroot proves the `Build-Depends` line is complete: nothing else is present in the chroot.

### `sbuild` with `dpkg-buildpackage` and `debhelper` (`make build`)

`dpkg-buildpackage` turns a [source package](#source-package) into packages by running its `debian/rules` through debhelper's `dh` sequence. sbuild runs that build inside the clean chroot. In unshare mode sbuild unpacks the chroot tarball into an unprivileged user namespace, installs only the package's declared build dependencies into that copy, builds, and copies the results out to `build/`. No root is involved, and a build that works proves the `Build-Depends` line is complete. `sbuild.conf` in the repo selects unshare mode and stops sbuild from recreating the chroot on its own. The results land in `build/`: the `.deb` binaries, a `.dsc` plus tarball (the source package), a `.changes` file listing every produced file, and a `.buildinfo` record of the build environment.

```
make build                                  # every directory under packages/
make build PKG=packages/hybrid-backlight    # one package
make build PKG=packages/quickshell          # a third-party package: downloads its upstream tarball into build/ first
UPSTREAM_DIR=~/Projects/omarchy make build PKG=packages/desktop   # the Omarchy port from the local checkout (without UPSTREAM_DIR: from the fork's ref)
WITH_REPO=1 make build                      # also enable our published repo in the chroot,
                                            # for packages that build-depend on ours
```

### `lintian` (runs inside `make build`)

lintian checks finished packages against Debian policy: file placement, maintainer scripts, descriptions, licenses, and rules such as "only a package named `*-apt-source` may install an apt sources file". `scripts/build.sh` runs lintian on every `.changes` file after the build and fails on errors. The `*-apt-source` rule is why the first source package produces two binaries, `@DISTRO_ID@-archive-keyring` and `@DISTRO_ID@-apt-source`.

### `gpg` (`scripts/keygen.sh`, once)

apt only trusts a repository whose `Release` file is signed by a key the system already has. `scripts/keygen.sh` creates our archive's signing key in your GnuPG home (RSA 4096, sign-only, two-year expiry), exports the public half into `packages/archive-keyring/`, and writes the fingerprint into `distro.conf`. The secret key never leaves the workstation; CI builds but does not sign. Run the script yourself in a terminal, because gpg asks for the apt signing key passphrase through pinentry.

```
scripts/keygen.sh
```

### `aptly` (`make publish`)

aptly manages apt repositories. aptly keeps a database and a package pool under `~/.local/state/@DISTRO_ID@/aptly`, and a *publish* turns a snapshot of the aptly repository into the `dists/` and `pool/` tree apt reads, with the `Release` file signed by the archive signing key from the gpg section. Snapshots make each publish atomic and let you switch the published tree back to an earlier snapshot. `scripts/repo-publish.sh` adds the newest version of each package in `build/` to the aptly repository (by dpkg's version order) and removes the older versions, so the repository offers one version of each, then removes what this tree no longer builds (a package whose source has no directory under `packages/`, such as `kpmcore` after its rebuild was dropped, and a binary package dropped from a control file, such as `@DISTRO_ID@-desktop-apps`, whose files are left from an older version of their source), takes a snapshot, publishes that snapshot with `Origin: @APT_ORIGIN@`, and, with `--push`, uploads the published tree to the S3 bucket `@APT_S3_BUCKET@` with the aws-cli. The bucket and its CloudFormation stack are described in [infra/apt-repo/README.md](../infra/apt-repo/README.md) (`make apt-infra` creates them once).

```
make publish                        # add build/'s newest packages, snapshot, publish, list what an upload would change
make publish PUBLISH_FLAGS=--push   # the same, then upload to the S3 bucket
```

Before adding anything, the script fetches the published index and refuses a `.deb` whose name and version are already published with different content: apt identifies a package by name and version, so installed systems would never see the new file. The fix is a version bump, which `make check-versions` asks for.

The published repository's files are in the S3 bucket `@APT_S3_BUCKET@`, and Amazon CloudFront serves them at `@APT_URL@` ([infra/apt-repo/README.md](../infra/apt-repo/README.md), which also says what CloudFront caches; [docs/2](2%20-%20build-infra.md), choice 10). An installed system reaches the repository through the `@DISTRO_ID@-apt-source` package, whose sources entry binds the repository to the key from `@DISTRO_ID@-archive-keyring`.

### `mmdebstrap` again (`make rootfs`)

The same program as `make chroot`, with the `minbase` variant instead of `buildd`, and the output is the distro's root filesystem rather than a build environment. `scripts/rootfs-create.sh` points mmdebstrap at the `@BASE_SERIES@` archive with the `-updates` and `-security` pockets and the `main`, `restricted` and `universe` components, has it install the three `.deb` files of ours from `build/` (the keyring, the apt source and `@DISTRO_ID@-base`), and lets the base metapackage's dependency list pull in the kernel, shim, GRUB, systemd, AppArmor, NetworkManager and PipeWire. Hooks write the hostname, the resolver symlink and apt's `ubuntu.sources`. The result is `build/rootfs/@DISTRO_ID@-@BASE_VERSION@-amd64-<profile>.tar` and a manifest of every installed package, where the profile chooses which of our packages go in: `PROFILE=base` for step 3's set, `session` for step 4's set on top of it, `desktop` for step 6's set on top of that, and `live` for step 7's set on top of that (casper, `@DISTRO_ID@-live` and `@DISTRO_ID@-installer`, with the initramfs rebuilt so casper's scripts are in it). `live` is the default because the default is always the newest layer, the one the ISO is made from; the `desktop` and `live` profiles also enable `multiverse`. No disk, partition or bootloader is touched: that is the image step. The packages apt downloads are kept in `build/apt-cache/` and copied into the next run's chroot, so each build downloads only what Ubuntu updated since the last one ([docs/3](3%20-%20rootfs.md), choice 10).

```
make rootfs                        # needs build/*.deb from make build; PROFILE=live, the ISO's live layer
make rootfs PROFILE=desktop        # step 6's tarball, without casper and the installer: the ISO's installed system
make rootfs PROFILE=session        # step 4's tarball
make rootfs PROFILE=base           # step 3's tarball
make rootfs PROFILE=desktop SOURCE=published   # step 6's tarball with our packages from the published archive
UBUNTU_MIRROR=http://us.archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu make rootfs
```

### `qemu-system-x86_64` with `mke2fs` (`make rootfs-test`)

QEMU is the machine emulator; with KVM (`/dev/kvm`) it runs the guest on the real CPU. `scripts/rootfs-boot-test.sh` pulls the kernel and initramfs out of the root filesystem tarball with `tar`, unpacks the tarball under `fakeroot` (a program that records file owners and modes in its own database and reports them to the programs it runs, so the files on disk stay the user's) and turns it into a raw ext4 disk image with `mke2fs -d`, which writes the recorded owners into the image, drops in a oneshot systemd unit that runs the checks and powers off, then boots the kernel directly (`-kernel`, `-initrd`, `-append`) with the ext4 image as `/dev/vda` and the serial console logged to a file. The script prints the checks (systemd running, no failed units, `os-release` identity, AppArmor and NetworkManager active, network connected, `apt update` succeeding against both archives, snapd available from the archive) and exits non-zero if any check failed. For the `session` profile the guest also gets a virtio GPU (with virgl through the host's render node when one is writable) and a test user that SDDM logs in automatically, and the checks continue: uwsm's units active in the user's systemd manager, Hyprland on the virtual monitor, `xeyes` as an Xwayland client, the three portals answering, the Secret Service (GNOME Keyring) answering, `ubuntu-drivers` and `@DISTRO_ID@-drivers-install --show` running. For the `desktop` profile the test user's home is seeded from `/etc/skel`, `@DISTRO_ID@-apply-system` runs before SDDM as the installer will run it, SDDM logs the user into the port's own session, and the checks continue with the shell the port launched (its IPC ping, its bar and background layers), first-run (the theme it set, the user units it enabled), a live theme switch, the menu, the fonts and the third-party packages. Then the bar must show its widgets (the shell's `debugBarGeometry` call; a bar whose plugins failed to load still has its layer surface), and the keybindings are checked: Lua, installed in the guest with apt, runs the test user's `hyprland.lua` through `scripts/boot-test-bindings.lua`, which lists every binding and the command it runs; `scripts/boot-test-bindings.py`, started through Hyprland so it sees the environment a key press gives a command, checks that every program those commands run is on `PATH`; and each binding that opens a menu or a shell panel is run through Hyprland (`hyprctl dispatch` with `hl.dsp.exec_cmd`), as a key press runs it, and must exit 0 and, for a menu, put the menu's layer on screen.

```
make rootfs-test                   # the live tarball with the desktop checks (casper's medium check masked, the installer's autostart removed: both need the ISO)
make rootfs-test PROFILE=desktop   # step 6's tarball, the same checks
make rootfs-test PROFILE=session   # steps 4 and 5's checks
make rootfs-test PROFILE=base      # step 3's checks only
KEEP=1 make rootfs-test            # keep build/rootfs/boot-test/ with the serial log after a pass too (a failure always keeps it)
```

### `mksquashfs`, `mkfs.vfat` with `mtools`, and `xorriso` (`make iso`)

`scripts/iso-create.sh` reads two tarballs of the same build, the desktop one (`make rootfs PROFILE=desktop`) and the live one (`make rootfs`, whose default profile is `live`: the desktop plus casper, `@DISTRO_ID@-live` and `@DISTRO_ID@-installer`), and writes `build/iso/@DISTRO_ID@-@BASE_VERSION@-amd64.iso`. Before the image is written, `scripts/driver-pool.sh` extracts the desktop tarball's apt configuration, dpkg status file and Ubuntu's archive keyring, runs the host's apt as the user against those files (`APT_CONFIG`, so apt resolves against the tarball's installed packages and preferences, with no chroot), asks apt for the newest NVIDIA open branch with prebuilt modules for the tarball's kernel, downloads that branch's packages and the root filesystem tools the installer adds (`btrfs-progs`, `timeshift`, `xfsprogs`), and lays them with a `dpkg-scanpackages` index and the NVIDIA license under `pool/` at the medium's root, where the installer's steps find them (`docs/7 - image-installer.md`, choices 20 and 55). mksquashfs compresses the desktop tarball straight from standard input into `casper/filesystem.squashfs` (no extraction, no root), the system the installer copies onto the disk; `scripts/layer-diff.py` writes what the live tarball has on top of the desktop one as a tar, which mksquashfs turns into `casper/filesystem.live.squashfs`, the layer casper stacks above the first for the live session (`layerfs-path=` on the kernel command line); the kernel and the initramfs, rebuilt with casper's hooks inside the live tarball, go to `casper/`; a FAT image made with mkfs.vfat and filled with mtools holds Ubuntu's Microsoft-signed shim, Canonical-signed CD GRUB and MokManager, taken from the live tarball's `shim-signed` and `grub-efi-amd64-signed`; xorriso writes the ISO 9660 image with that FAT image appended as an EFI System partition. A second image, `-test.iso`, is the same ISO with a GRUB entry that wakes the boot test; xorriso copies the first and replaces one file. UEFI only.

### `qemu-system-x86_64` with OVMF (`make iso-test`)

`scripts/iso-boot-test.sh` boots the test ISO under OVMF, Ubuntu's UEFI firmware for QEMU: `OVMF_CODE_4M.secboot.fd` with Microsoft's keys enrolled (`SECURE_BOOT=on`, the default) or the same firmware with no keys enrolled (`SECURE_BOOT=off`). The guest's test unit, from `@DISTRO_ID@-live`, prints its checks on the serial console; the script compares them: Secure Boot state, casper, the live user, the session with the shell, the installer running with its window.

### `qemu-system-x86_64` with OVMF, four boots (`make factory-reset-test`)

`scripts/factory-reset-test.sh` checks the factory reset (docs/6, choice 87) end to end in four QEMU runs under OVMF with Secure Boot on, sharing one firmware variable file and a sparse 40 GiB disk. A Python watcher on the guest's serial console types the disk encryption passphrase when the initramfs asks and turns `=== KEYS` lines into key presses. Boot A is the test ISO: after the live session's own checks, `tests/factory-reset/run.sh` installs the distro on the disk with the encrypted btrfs layout the installer's settings describe and the installer's own scripts, ending with `factory-snapshot`; Calamares has no unattended mode, so this is a scripted install, not the installer's pages. Boot B unlocks the disk with the first owner's passphrase and runs `@DISTRO_ID@-system-factory-reset` with its prompts answered by a stand-in for gum. Boot C must not ask for a passphrase: the reset's wipe runs, the owner form takes its answers from the stand-in, and `check.sh` prints the users, groups, files, LUKS key slots, `crypttab`, conf-hook, initramfs, subvolumes, swap, hostname, timezone, keyboard and login. Boot D unlocks with the new owner's password and checks that the one-boot autologin is gone. Logs stay in `build/factory-reset-test/` when the test fails.

```
make factory-reset-test           # needs the test ISO from make iso, and KVM
```

### `unshare` with `chroot` (`make drivers-check`)

`unshare` opens an unprivileged user namespace with its own mount and pid namespaces; `chroot` runs a command with the extracted tarball as its root. `scripts/drivers-check.sh` extracts the root filesystem tarball, bind-mounts the host's `/sys` and `/dev` into it, mounts a fresh `/proc`, then runs `apt-get update`, `ubuntu-drivers devices`, `@DISTRO_ID@-drivers-install --show` and an `apt-get --simulate install` of the list that command would hand apt, all without root privileges. The script prints what Ubuntu's detector recommends for this machine's GPU, which branch `@DISTRO_ID@-drivers-install` chooses instead (the newest NVIDIA open branch with prebuilt modules), and checks that the choice resolves to Canonical's prebuilt signed NVIDIA modules, not DKMS.

```
make drivers-check
KEEP=1 make drivers-check          # keep build/rootfs/drivers-check/root
```

### `git` with `dpkg --compare-versions` (`make check-versions`)

`scripts/version-check.sh` finds the newest `v*` tag reachable from `HEAD`, and for every directory under `packages/` that differs from that tag it compares the first line of the directory's `debian/changelog` at `HEAD` with the same line at the tag; the version must rank higher. A directory that did not change needs no bump, and a package new since the tag passes. Before the first tag the script prints that there is nothing to compare against and passes.

```
make check-versions
VERSION_CHECK_TAG=<ref> scripts/version-check.sh   # compare against any ref
```

### `mmdebstrap` with `dpkg-scanpackages` (`make upgrade-test`)

`scripts/upgrade-test.sh` is the N to N+1 test: N is the set of versions the published archive at `@APT_URL@` offers, N+1 is `build/`. One mmdebstrap run (unshare mode, output `/dev/null`) installs the `minbase` variant plus, from the published archive, every package name that is both published and in `build/`; a script then runs inside the chroot: it creates a user from N's `/etc/skel` with a marker line in one of the user's files, points apt at `build/` (copied in as a flat file archive indexed by `dpkg-scanpackages`), runs `apt-get full-upgrade` with the options `@DISTRO_ID@-update -y` uses, records versions, `dpkg --audit`, what apt would still upgrade and the unattended-upgrades origins, and, when `@DISTRO_ID@-tools` is installed, drops a synthetic migration and runs `@DISTRO_ID@-migrate` as the user. The report lands in `build/upgrade-test/report.txt` and the script compares it with its expectations. The run uses the same package cache as `make rootfs`, `build/apt-cache/`.

```
make upgrade-test                  # needs build/*.deb and network access to the published archive
```

### `qemu-system-x86_64` on the published desktop (`make session-upgrade-test`)

The upgrade test above upgrades packages in a chroot, where no desktop runs. An update on an installed system happens inside a running session, whose environment, shell process and Hyprland configuration all started on N; on 2026-10-17 an update that renamed `OAXACA_PATH` to `OMARCHY_PATH` left the running session's bar empty and Super+Space dead, and no test saw it, because every boot test booted a fresh image. `make session-upgrade-test` runs two scripts. `scripts/rootfs-create.sh` with `SOURCE=published` builds the desktop tarball with our packages from the published archive instead of `build/` (`build/rootfs/@DISTRO_ID@-@BASE_VERSION@-amd64-desktop-published.tar`). `scripts/rootfs-boot-test.sh` with `UPGRADE=1` boots that tarball as `make rootfs-test` boots its own, with `build/`'s newest packages copied into the image as a flat file archive. Once first-run has finished and the shell answers, the guest adds apt's source for that archive and runs `@DISTRO_ID@-update -y` as the test user in the environment the session had at login, as a terminal opened before the update would. The update must exit 0, print no shell errors (an unbound variable, a missing command or file, Omarchy's "Something went wrong"), change the desktop's version and restart the shell. Then every desktop check above runs against the upgraded system inside the session that began before the update.

```
make session-upgrade-test          # needs build/*.deb, KVM, and network access to the published archive
```

### `curl` with GitHub's and Launchpad's APIs (`make upstream-check`)

`scripts/upstream-check.sh` covers the packages that repackage a program's GitHub release, the ones whose `upstream` file sets `RELEASES=<owner>/<repo>` (today `packages/asusctl`, `packages/localsend`, `packages/ttfx` and `packages/voxtype`). For each one it asks GitHub's API for the repository's latest release (drafts and pre-releases skipped), drops a leading `v` from the release's tag and compares it with the upstream part of the version in the package's `debian/changelog`. A newer release fails the check and names the file to update; a package can sit behind its upstream unnoticed otherwise, and LocalSend receives files over the network. The weekly workflow `.github/workflows/upstream-check.yml` (Mondays 14:00 UTC, plus a "Run workflow" button) runs the same target, so a new release turns up as GitHub's failed-workflow notification. Exit status: 0 current, 1 a newer release, 2 GitHub could not be asked.

```
make upstream-check
-- asusctl: 6.5.0 is current (OpenGamingCollective/asusctl's latest release is 6.5.0)
-- localsend: 1.18.2 is current (localsend/localsend's latest release is 1.18.2)
-- ttfx: 0.5.0 is current (omacom/ttfx's latest release is 0.5.0)
!! voxtype: peteonrails/voxtype released 1.1.1; this repository builds 1.1.0.
   Update the version and the SHA-256s in packages/voxtype/upstream, add a changelog entry, make build PKG=packages/voxtype
```

That is the output of 2026-10-18; the GitHub check's lines come first.

The same command also covers the Ubuntu source packages this repository rebuilds with a patch, the ones whose `upstream` file sets `DSC=` to a source file on Launchpad (today `packages/plymouth` and `packages/calamares`). For each one it asks Launchpad's API which versions of that source package are published in `@BASE_SERIES@`, and fails when the release, `-updates` or `-security` pocket has a version above the one the rebuild starts from (the version in the `DSC=` URL). A rebuild's version ranks below Ubuntu's next upload ([docs/1, Version scheme](1%20-%20policy.md)), so apt replaces the rebuild with Ubuntu's package once the upload is published, and the distro's patch survives only if Ubuntu's upload carries it. Since `@BASE_SERIES@`'s release on 2026-10-15, such an upload is a stable update. A version only in `-proposed` is printed and does not fail the check, since apt does not install from `-proposed`. The same run's last lines:

```
-- calamares: 3.3.14-0ubuntu30 is Ubuntu's newest calamares in stonking
-- plymouth: 24.004.60+git20250831.4a3c171d-0ubuntu10 is Ubuntu's newest plymouth in stonking
```

A newer Ubuntu upload of a rebuilt package: read its changelog for the distro's patch (the patch's upstream commit, named in the patch's DEP-3 header). If the upload carries the fix, the rebuild can go, since apt already prefers Ubuntu's package. If not, rebase: in the package's `upstream`, point `DSC=` at the new `.dsc` and set `SHA256=` from `sha256sum` of the download, add a changelog entry with the new base version, `make build PKG=packages/<package>`, then publish.

A new LocalSend release: in `packages/localsend/upstream`, change the version in both URLs and both SHA-256s (GitHub shows each file's SHA-256 on the release page), add a changelog entry, `make build PKG=packages/localsend`, then publish. A new ttfx release: see `packages/ttfx/README.md`. A new Voxtype release: in `packages/voxtype/sources.list`, change the version in the URL and the SHA-256, add a changelog entry, `make build PKG=packages/voxtype`, then publish.

### GitHub Actions (`.github/workflows/build.yml`)

Both workflows, `build` and `upstream-check`, are disabled on GitHub since 2026-10-19 (the workflow files are unchanged); turning them back on is in [To do](#to-do).

On every push, pull request and `v*` tag, four jobs run on `ubuntu-26.04` runners. `prepare` runs `make check-render` and `make check-versions`, lists the directories under `packages/` and runs `make chroot` once (cached weekly). `build` runs one job per package directory, in parallel: a job whose package directory, `distro.conf`, build scripts and chroot week are unchanged since its last successful build restores that build from the cache and uploads it; otherwise it installs sbuild, mmdebstrap and cargo and runs `make build PKG=packages/<directory>` with `TMPDIR=/var/tmp` (sbuild unpacks the chroot and builds under `/tmp` otherwise, a 3.9 GB tmpfs on the runner; `quickshell` needs 4.8 GB there) and `DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=parallel=2` (the runner has 16 GB; `quickshell` peaks at 12.4 GB with four compiler jobs and 1.9 GB with two). Each job uploads its packages and sbuild's build log as the artifact `packages-<directory>`. `upgrade-test` gathers every artifact into `build/` and runs `make upgrade-test` against the published archive. On a tag, `release` gathers every artifact and attaches the packages to a GitHub Release. The runners use the same toolchain as the workstation, so a green run means the packages build from a clean checkout. Publishing stays on the workstation, where the signing key is.

### `scripts/suspend-baseline.sh` (measurement, not build)

Before the distro existed, the Legion laptop's suspend behaviour was measured on its current Ubuntu install so that a later sleep failure on the new distro can be blamed on the distro or on the hardware. The script records device state, sets an RTC wake alarm with `rtcwake -m no`, runs `systemctl suspend`, waits for the kernel's `PM: suspend exit` line, and records device state and the relevant journal lines again. The same script will run the plan's sleep tests on the new distro.

```
scripts/suspend-baseline.sh --label lid-open-docked
scripts/suspend-baseline.sh --label lid-closed-docked --delay 30
scripts/suspend-baseline.sh --snapshot          # just print device state
```

## How the pieces fit together

```
distro.conf ──render──▶ packages/*/debian/*, README.md, ...        (make render)

Ubuntu archive ──mmdebstrap──▶ ~/.cache/sbuild/@BASE_SERIES@-amd64.tar  (make chroot)
                                        │
packages/<dir>/ ──sbuild (dpkg-buildpackage + dh, inside the chroot)──▶ build/*.deb, *.dsc
                                        │                                   (make build)
                                     lintian
                                        │
build/ ──aptly (snapshot, publish, sign with the gpg key)──▶ ~/.local/state/@DISTRO_ID@/aptly/public/dists, pool
                                                                            (make publish)
                                        │
                                aws s3 sync ──▶ S3 bucket @APT_S3_BUCKET@ ──CloudFront──▶ @APT_URL@
                                                                      │
                     installed systems: @DISTRO_ID@-apt-source + @DISTRO_ID@-archive-keyring ──▶ apt update

~/Projects/omarchy (the fork's oaxaca branch) ──git archive, rename at build──▶ build/@DISTRO_ID@-{desktop,tools,shell,themes,config}*.deb   (make build PKG=packages/desktop)

Ubuntu archive + build/{archive-keyring,apt-source,base[,nvidia,session,quickshell[,fonts,desktop,tools,shell,themes,config,asdcontrol,hyprland-preview-share-picker,nwg-displays,plymouth,libplymouth5,plymouth-label[,live,installer]]]}.deb ──mmdebstrap (minbase)──▶ build/rootfs/@DISTRO_ID@-@BASE_VERSION@-amd64-<profile>.tar
                                                                                        │        (make rootfs PROFILE=base|session|desktop|live)
                                                tar + mke2fs (+ Omarchy's shell/ for the session profile; /etc/skel and apply-system for desktop) ──▶ ext4 image ──qemu──▶ boot checks   (make rootfs-test)
                                                tar + unshare/chroot ──▶ ubuntu-drivers devices, @DISTRO_ID@-drivers-install --show, apt --simulate   (make drivers-check)

@APT_URL@ (N) + build/*.deb (N+1) ──mmdebstrap (minbase + N), apt-get full-upgrade, @DISTRO_ID@-migrate──▶ build/upgrade-test/report.txt   (make upgrade-test; CI)
@APT_URL@ (N, desktop tarball) + build/*.deb (N+1) ──qemu: log in on N, @DISTRO_ID@-update -y inside the session──▶ desktop checks on N+1   (make session-upgrade-test)

desktop tarball (the installed system) + live tarball (PROFILE=live: desktop + casper + @DISTRO_ID@-live + @DISTRO_ID@-installer) + the NVIDIA driver pool (apt download) ──mksquashfs (two layers: the desktop, then what the live tarball adds), signed shim and GRUB into a FAT image, xorriso──▶ build/iso/@DISTRO_ID@-@BASE_VERSION@-amd64.iso (+ -test.iso)   (make iso)
                                                                                        │
                                                              qemu + OVMF (Secure Boot on, then off) ──▶ live session checks   (make iso-test)
```

CI runs the first three rows (render, chroot, build) and the upgrade test on every push and hands the packages to a GitHub Release; the workstation runs the publish and push rows and the root filesystem rows.

## Building the distro from source

Going from a fresh clone of this repository to a bootable USB stick is a chain of `make` targets, then one `dd`. Every target is described above; this list gives the order the targets run in on a clean checkout and what each one leaves behind. Nothing here needs root except the final write to the stick. Two targets are missing on purpose. `make render` is absent because every rendered file is committed next to its `.in` template (CI's `make check-render` keeps the two in step), so a clone is complete, and `make build` runs `render` first anyway. `make publish` is absent because no step reads the published repository or the secret signing key: `make rootfs` installs our packages from the `.deb` files in `build/` and writes only Ubuntu's apt sources into the tarball, and the one key file the build needs, the public `packages/archive-keyring/archive-keyring.gpg`, is committed. Publishing matters after installation: the installed system's apt sources entry points at `@APT_URL@`, so `apt update` on an installed machine needs the repository published, which is the "Releasing" line under Day-to-day.

1. Install the tools. `make machine-setup SETUP_FLAGS=--tools` runs `scripts/machine-setup.sh`, which installs them from Ubuntu's archive (the list, with what each tool is for, is at the top of the script) and stops; without `SETUP_FLAGS` the script goes on to the publishing setup in [Releasing](#releasing). `lua5.5` and `ripgrep` are not for the build: the Omarchy fork's own tests (`./test/all` in the fork's checkout; the fork's `PORT.md`, "Tests") need `lua5.5`'s `lua` command to read the fork's Hyprland configuration, which is written in Lua, `ripgrep`'s `rg` for three more test files, and `nodejs`'s `node` for the 47 test files that run JavaScript (the shell's `Model.js` files among them). `qt6-declarative-dev-tools` is not for the build or the tests either: `qt6-declarative-dev-tools` provides `/usr/lib/qt6/bin/qmllint`, which checks the fork's shell QML by hand:

   ```sh
   make machine-setup SETUP_FLAGS=--tools
   ```

2. `make chroot`: the clean build [chroot](#chroot) tarball under `~/.cache/sbuild/`, about 25 minutes the first time, kept for a week afterwards.
3. `make build`: every [source package](#source-package) under `packages/`, in a fresh copy of the chroot each, into `build/*.deb`. No package of ours build-depends on another, so one run builds all of them in any order. `quickshell` is the slow one (16 minutes on a 4-CPU CI runner).
4. `make rootfs PROFILE=desktop`, then `make rootfs`: mmdebstrap assembles the desktop root filesystem, the system the ISO installs, into `build/rootfs/@DISTRO_ID@-@BASE_VERSION@-amd64-desktop.tar`, then the live root filesystem (`live` is the default profile) from the Ubuntu archive plus `build/*.deb` into `build/rootfs/@DISTRO_ID@-@BASE_VERSION@-amd64-live.tar` (3.6 GB, 1188 packages on 2026-10-10), rebuilding the initramfs inside the live tarball so casper's scripts are in the initramfs the ISO boots.
5. `make iso`: the two tarballs become `build/iso/@DISTRO_ID@-@BASE_VERSION@-amd64.iso`, the desktop as the installed system's squashfs and the rest of the live tarball as a squashfs layer above it (docs/7, choice 55), plus `-test.iso` for the next step. This step also downloads the NVIDIA driver's packages from Ubuntu's archive into a pool on the medium (`scripts/driver-pool.sh`, a few hundred MB, so the install works offline; `DRIVER_POOL=0 make iso` leaves them out for a quick build).
6. `make iso-test`, then `make iso-test SECURE_BOOT=off`: the test ISO boots in QEMU under OVMF and the live session's checks print on the serial console; each run ends with `ISO boot test passed`. Optional, but the only automated check of the image.
7. Write the ISO to a USB stick. The image is a disk image, not just a CD image: xorriso wrote a protective MBR and a GPT with two partitions, the ISO 9660 filesystem as partition 1 and the FAT image with shim and GRUB as partition 2, type EFI System (`fdisk -l build/iso/@DISTRO_ID@-@BASE_VERSION@-amd64.iso` lists them). A raw copy of the image onto a stick therefore gives the firmware a GPT disk with an EFI System partition to boot from. Find the stick's device, then copy; everything on the stick is erased:

   ```
   lsblk -d -o NAME,SIZE,MODEL,TRAN                   # the stick is the "usb" row; say it is /dev/sdX
   sudo dd if=build/iso/@DISTRO_ID@-@BASE_VERSION@-amd64.iso of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress conv=fsync
   ```

   `conv=fsync` makes `dd` wait until the stick has the data before it exits. Any program that writes a raw image to a device does the same job (GNOME Disks' "Restore Disk Image", `cp image /dev/sdX`); the ISO is not re-packed by any of them.
8. Boot the stick with the firmware's boot menu, Secure Boot on or off. The live session logs in as `@DISTRO_ID@` and opens the installer; the installer's pages and what runs after them are in [docs/7 - image-installer.md](7%20-%20image-installer.md). This last step is verified in QEMU only so far; the first run on the Legion laptop is carried in that document.

Rebuilding after a change repeats the chain from the step whose input changed: a package edit is steps 3 to 7 (`make build PKG=packages/<dir>` for just that package), a change to the fork's branch is the same with `PKG=packages/desktop`, and an ISO-only change (the GRUB menu, the EFI image) is steps 5 to 7.

## Day-to-day

The first time on a new workstation: `make machine-setup` ([Releasing, Once per machine](#once-per-machine)). Creating the signing key, the GitHub repository and the apt repository's S3 bucket (`make apt-infra`) are one-time steps described in [docs/2 - build-infra.md](2%20-%20build-infra.md) and [infra/apt-repo/README.md](../infra/apt-repo/README.md).

Adding a package: create `packages/<dir>` with a `debian/` directory, using `.in` templates wherever the distro's name or identifier appears, and debhelper's unprefixed file names (`debian/install`, `debian/service`, `debian/udev`), then `make build PKG=packages/<dir>` until lintian is quiet. A third-party package we rebuild (`packages/quickshell`) holds only `debian/` and an `upstream` file with the tarball's URL and SHA-256; `scripts/build.sh` downloads the tarball into `build/` and unpacks it around `debian/` before `dpkg-source`. The `upstream` file can instead name a git tree (`GIT=`, `REF=`; `packages/desktop`) or a list of files (`FILES=`; `packages/fonts`), or an Ubuntu source package rebuilt with our additions (`DSC=`; `packages/plymouth`, whose `debian/` holds only its patch, its series line and a changelog entry, laid over Ubuntu's `debian/` at build time, so a one-line fix to an archive package needs no fork and no copy of its packaging), and add component tarballs and `CARGO_VENDOR=1` (`packages/hyprland-preview-share-picker`); the header of `scripts/build.sh` documents each. Directory names never carry the distro name. Versions follow the scheme in [docs/1 - policy.md](1%20-%20policy.md).

Releasing: the hand edits in [Before each release](#before-each-release), `make release-message` to see what the release will build, then `make release` ([Releasing](#releasing)).

## Shipping a change: the apt repository or a new ISO

A change reaches users one of two ways. The installation ISO (the [live medium](7%20-%20image-installer.md#live-medium-and-casper) built by `make iso`) is what a new install is made from; the apt repository at `@APT_URL@` (filled by `make publish PUBLISH_FLAGS=--push`) is what an installed system updates from, through `@DISTRO_ID@-update`, the port of Omarchy's update command (`apt-get update`, `apt-get full-upgrade`, then the [migrations](8%20-%20update-path.md#migration), the user's hooks, the cleanup and the reboot offer; [docs/8 - update-path.md](8%20-%20update-path.md)).

**Through the apt repository, no new ISO:** any change inside a [package](#package) that is installed on the system: the desktop (`@DISTRO_ID@-desktop`, `-tools`, `-shell`, `-themes`, `-config`, built from the Omarchy fork), `@DISTRO_ID@-base`, `@DISTRO_ID@-session`, `@DISTRO_ID@-nvidia`, `@DISTRO_ID@-hw-legion`, and the Ubuntu packages their dependency lists add. The package's version has to be bumped (`make check-versions`), then built and published; installed systems get it with `@DISTRO_ID@-update`. What a package install alone cannot change, such as files already in users' home folders (`/etc/skel` only seeds new users), ships as a migration in the fork's `migrations/`, which `@DISTRO_ID@-update` runs after the packages.

**Only through a new ISO:**
1. The installer: Calamares (our rebuild) and KPMcore (Ubuntu's), `@DISTRO_ID@-installer` (its pages and settings, `keep-previous-loader`, `hardware-install`, `sddm-last-user`) and the install-time steps (`@DISTRO_ID@-apply-system --first-install`, the drivers and hardware steps). They run only from the medium, so a fix helps the next install only.
2. The live session: `@DISTRO_ID@-live`, the live user and what the live desktop starts.
3. The medium's boot setup: its GRUB menu, kernel command line and EFI System Partition image.
4. The medium's package pool (`/cdrom/pool`, `scripts/driver-pool.sh`): the NVIDIA driver branch and the hardware packages the installer installs without a network.
5. Anything that has to be right on the first boot after an install, before an update can run: an installed system that cannot boot, or whose sources entry names the wrong repository URL (`@DISTRO_ID@-apt-source`), never receives the fix through apt. The initramfs hook that put the disk encryption passphrase prompt on a screenless GPU (docs/7, choice 39) was such a change.
6. Every new install: it gets the ISO's package versions. With a network, `@DISTRO_ID@-update` brings it up to date right after, but a defect that bites before that first update stays. So an ISO is offered for installing only when it was built after every change in this repository and the fork, and its package versions are the published ones.

**In practice:** a package change is bump, `make build PKG=packages/<dir>`, `make publish PUBLISH_FLAGS=--push`, then `@DISTRO_ID@-update` on the installed system; before the next install, `make rootfs PROFILE=desktop && make rootfs && make iso && make iso-test` from the same sources. [Releasing](#releasing) does both in one pass.

## Releasing

A release is one upload of the apt repository at `@APT_URL@`, made after the changed packages are built and the image built from them has passed its boot tests. Installed systems receive a release through `@DISTRO_ID@-update`, and the ISO the release builds on the way is the one to offer for the next install.

The upload's anatomy: `make publish` runs `scripts/repo-publish.sh`, which drives two programs. aptly keeps the repository's database and package pool under `~/.local/state/@DISTRO_ID@/aptly` on the workstation, adds `build/`'s newest packages to that database, and signs the published tree with the apt signing key (section [`aptly`](#aptly-make-publish)). The aws-cli's `aws s3 sync` then uploads aptly's published tree to the S3 bucket `@APT_S3_BUCKET@`, deleting from the bucket whatever aptly's tree no longer has.

Two scripts run the whole process; each one's header comment says what the script runs and in what order:

- `scripts/machine-setup.sh` (`make machine-setup`), once per workstation;
- `scripts/release.sh` (`make release`), once per release.

Both expect this repository cloned at `~/Projects/oaxaca` and the Omarchy fork at `~/Projects/omarchy` (branch `@OMARCHY_PORT_REF@`; `OMARCHY_FORK_DIR=<path>` names another checkout), and these credentials in place:

- an SSH key that GitHub accepts for both repositories, for `git push`;
- the apt signing key in GnuPG, restored from the password manager as in [docs/2, Backing up the signing key](2%20-%20build-infra.md#backing-up-the-signing-key);
- an aws-cli profile named as `APT_AWS_PROFILE` in `distro.conf`, allowed to write to the bucket `@APT_S3_BUCKET@`;
- write access to `/dev/kvm`, for QEMU's boot tests.

### Once per machine

```sh
cd ~/Projects/oaxaca && make machine-setup
```

What `make machine-setup` does, stopping at the first failure:

1. Installs the build, test and publish tools with apt (aptly is in universe), and the aws-cli from Amazon's snap.
2. Checks each credential above, plus subordinate ids in `/etc/subuid` for mmdebstrap's unshare mode, and lists every one that is missing.
3. Builds the clean build chroot (`scripts/chroot-create.sh`, the same as `make chroot`).
4. Seeds aptly's database and `build/` from the published repository (`scripts/repo-publish.sh --seed`, the same as `make repo-seed`).

Step 4 matters most on a new machine. The new machine's aptly database starts empty, and `make publish` uploads only what that database holds, deleting the rest from the bucket: a first publish from `build/` alone would remove every package the release did not rebuild. The seed fills aptly's database through an aptly mirror (aptly's copy of a remote repository, checked against our archive keyring) and copies the published `.deb` files into `build/`, so that `make rootfs` has every package and `make publish`'s guard sees the published files byte for byte. `make publish` itself refuses to run on an empty aptly database while the repository is published, and names `make repo-seed`. Every step skips work already done, so a second run of `make machine-setup` only checks.

### Before each release

`make release` edits only the files it renders (step 2 of [Each release](#each-release)); everything else that a release needs changed is edited by hand first, in this order.

| Step | Files edited by hand | Where |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | the port's changes | the fork, `~/Projects/omarchy`, branch `@OMARCHY_PORT_REF@` |
| 2 | `packages/desktop/debian/changelog.in`, when the fork changed | this repository |
| 3 | `packages/<package>/debian/changelog.in` of every other package that changed, where `<package>` is the package's directory name (`installer`, `live`, ...) | this repository |
| 4 | the `docs/` documents that record each change, such as `docs/6 - omarchy-port.md`; `docs/README.md.in` where it describes changed behavior | this repository |

Files with a `.in` template are never edited directly: `make render` writes each one from its template, and both are committed (CI's `make check-render` fails when they differ).

1. **Commit the fork's changes** on the `@OMARCHY_PORT_REF@` branch in `~/Projects/omarchy` (`make release` pushes them, and refuses a fork with uncommitted changes).
2. **When the fork changed, give the desktop package a new changelog entry.** The top entry of `packages/desktop/debian/changelog.in` carries the placeholder `@@OMARCHY_PORT_VERSION@@`, which `make render` fills from `distro.conf`, so the top entry always names the version being built. Before `make release` moves `distro.conf` to the fork's new commit, write the published version into the top entry, while `distro.conf` still names that version:

   ```sh
   ( cd ~/Projects/oaxaca && source distro.conf && sed -i "1s/@@OMARCHY_PORT_VERSION@@/$OMARCHY_PORT_VERSION/" packages/desktop/debian/changelog.in && head -1 packages/desktop/debian/changelog.in )
   ```

   Then add a new entry above it, with the placeholder in the first line, one bullet per change, and a date from `date -R`:

   ```
   @@DISTRO_ID@@-desktop (@@OMARCHY_PORT_VERSION@@-0@@DISTRO_ID@@1) @@BASE_SERIES@@; urgency=medium

     * What changed, for users.

    -- @@MAINTAINER@@  Fri, 16 Oct 2026 18:00:00 -0500
   ```

   `make release` stops with this instruction when the fork moved and the entry is missing.
3. **Bump every other package that changed**: a new entry at the top of its `debian/changelog.in` with a higher version, following [docs/1, Version scheme](1%20-%20policy.md). A package changed without a new version is not built.
4. **Document each change, render, and commit this repository.** Each change gets its bullets in the changed packages' `debian/changelog.in` entries (steps 2 and 3) and, when it is a decision, a numbered choice in the `docs/` document of its roadmap step, with `docs/README.md.in` updated where it describes the changed behavior. Then render and commit:

   ```sh
   cd ~/Projects/oaxaca && make render && git add -A && git commit -m "<what changed>" && git push
   ```

   `<what changed>` is the commit's message, in your own words. `make release` commits with `git add -A` (its step 8), so anything left uncommitted goes into the release commit, mixed with the files the release itself rewrites.
5. **Print the release commit's message**, to check what the release will build before starting it:

   ```sh
   cd ~/Projects/oaxaca && make release-message
   ```

   `make release-message` runs `scripts/release.sh --message`, which computes the message the way `make release` does and changes nothing: it pushes nothing, writes no file and builds nothing. The output before the release of 2026-10-22 whose release commit is `a584c99`:

   ```
   Release: desktop 4.0.6+git20261022.128.a95cf5f7
   ```

   The desktop's version is computed from the fork's HEAD: `4.0.6` is Omarchy's tag (`OMARCHY_TAG` in `distro.conf`), then the commit's date in UTC, the number of the fork's commits since the tag, and the commit's short hash. Every other entry is a package directory whose version at the top of `debian/changelog` is not published yet, with that version. A package missing from the list will not be built, usually because step 3 was skipped. The command stops with "has uncommitted changes" when the fork has uncommitted changes, since a commit changes the hash, and prints "nothing to release" when every version is published.

### Each release

First check that Ubuntu's security servers serve the same `@BASE_SERIES@-security` index. `security.ubuntu.com` resolves to a pool of servers, and every root filesystem build asks one of them at random. While Ubuntu publishes a security update, some servers serve the new index and others still serve the old one, so the desktop root filesystem and the live one, built minutes apart, can get different versions of one package. `make iso` then stops with `the desktop and live root filesystems differ (package, desktop version, live version)`. On 2026-10-21 the release listed `libsoup-3.0-0   3.6.6-1ubuntu3  3.6.6-1`, after hours of building and testing. The command below asks each server in the pool for the `Date:` line of its `@BASE_SERIES@-security` index (`InRelease`) and prints each date with the number of servers serving it:

```sh
for ip in $(getent ahostsv4 security.ubuntu.com | awk '{print $1}' | sort -u); do curl -s --max-time 20 --resolve security.ubuntu.com:80:$ip http://security.ubuntu.com/ubuntu/dists/@BASE_SERIES@-security/InRelease | grep -m1 '^Date:'; done | sort | uniq -c
```

One line means every server agrees, and the release can start. Two or more lines mean an update is still spreading; wait and run the command again. The output on 2026-10-22 at 06:26 UTC, with one server behind:

```
      8 Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026  5:17:58 UTC
      1 Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 21:41:19 UTC
```

Then run the release:

```sh
cd ~/Projects/oaxaca && make release
```

What `make release` does, stopping at the first failure:

1. Pushes the fork, after checking it is on `@OMARCHY_PORT_REF@` with nothing uncommitted: the desktop build clones the fork's branch from GitHub, not the local checkout.
2. Sets `OMARCHY_PORT_DATE`, `_COUNT` and `_COMMIT` in `distro.conf` from the fork's HEAD (commit date in UTC, commits since `@OMARCHY_TAG@`, short hash: together the desktop's version) and runs `make render`. When the fork moved, the render rewrites four files, which the release commit (step 8) carries; none of them is edited by hand:
   - `distro.conf`: the three `OMARCHY_PORT_` values;
   - `packages/desktop/upstream`, from `upstream.in`: `DATE=`, `COUNT=` and `COMMIT=`, which `scripts/build.sh` checks against the commit it fetches;
   - `packages/desktop/debian/changelog`, from `changelog.in`: the top entry's version;
   - `packages/desktop/README.md`, from `README.md.in`: the desktop's version.
3. Builds the packages whose version is not published yet (`make build PKG=...`). Release tags (`v*`) have not been used so far, so `make check-versions` has nothing to compare against; instead, a package needs building when the version at the top of its rendered `debian/changelog` is missing from the published source index (`dists/@BASE_SERIES@/main/source/Sources.gz`). A package changed without a version bump is therefore not built. When every version is published, the release ends here with "nothing to release".
4. Runs `make upstream-check` and reports a newer upstream release without stopping.
5. Builds the live root filesystem and boots it in QEMU (`make rootfs`, `make rootfs-test`: the base, session and desktop checks).
6. Builds the ISO, after checking that the desktop and live root filesystems hold the same version of every package they share (the security servers' check above), and boots it under OVMF with Secure Boot on, then off (`make iso`, `make iso-test`, `make iso-test SECURE_BOOT=off`), then installs it on an encrypted btrfs disk and factory-resets it (`make factory-reset-test`).
7. Upgrades the published packages to `build/`'s in a throwaway chroot, migrations included (`make upgrade-test`), then boots the published desktop and updates it to `build/`'s inside its running session (`make session-upgrade-test`).
8. Commits this repository with `git add -A` and pushes. git opens the editor with the release commit's message already filled in, the same message `make release-message` prints (step 5 of [Before each release](#before-each-release)): saving the file accepts the message, and emptying the file stops the release. The message names the desktop's version and each other package step 3 built, short names without the `@DISTRO_ID@-` prefix, in the form of the commit of 2026-10-18:

   ```
   Release: desktop 4.0.4+git20261018.98.e07d28a1, installer 0.1.10, live 0.1.7, calamares 3.3.14-0ubuntu30oaxaca3
   ```

   The `Release:` prefix matters: since the ISO is built before this commit, the ISO is current when it is newer than the last commit that is not a release commit (`git log -1 --format=%ci --invert-grep --grep='^Release:'`).
9. Signs the repository locally with `make publish` (pinentry asks for the apt signing key passphrase), lists what the upload would change, and asks `Upload to @APT_S3_BUCKET@? [y/N]`.
10. On `y`, uploads with `make publish PUBLISH_FLAGS=--push` and checks the uploaded signature with `gpgv`, which must print `Good signature from "@DISTRO_NAME@ Archive Signing Key"` ([docs/2, One-time setup](2%20-%20build-infra.md#one-time-setup-in-order), step 5, explains the other results).
11. Deletes the builds this release superseded from `build/` with `make prune` (`scripts/build-prune.sh`), keeping the newest build of each package, which is the published one: older `.deb` files, older source packages with their `.changes`, `.buildinfo` and `.build` logs, and older unpacked source trees such as `build/@DISTRO_ID@-desktop-<version>/`. `build/`'s subfolders (`apt-cache/`, `rootfs/`, `iso/`, `git/`) stay. `make prune PRUNE_FLAGS=--dry-run` lists what it would delete.
12. Lists the ISO that step 6 built with `make iso-publish ISO_PUBLISH_FLAGS=--dry-run` (its [ISO version](../infra/iso/README.md#iso-version), checked against the ISO's own `.disk/info`, and the files an upload would write) and asks `Upload the ISO to @ISO_S3_BUCKET@ (@ISO_HOST@)? [y/N]`. On `y`, `make iso-publish` writes `SHA256SUMS`, signs it with the archive key (pinentry asks for the apt signing key passphrase unless gpg-agent still holds it from step 9), uploads the ISO, the checksums, the archive key's public half and `latest.json` to `@ISO_URL@`, deletes all but the newest @ISO_KEEP@ ISOs, and checks the signature and the size the host serves ([infra/iso/README.md](../infra/iso/README.md), docs/7 choice 59). On anything else the release ends there, and `make iso-publish` uploads the ISO later. After an upload, publish the website, whose build reads `latest.json` for its download button.

A release that stopped resumes with another `make release`: `distro.conf` already names the fork's commit, and the packages built so far are rebuilt. Until then, the four files of step 2 show as changed in `git status`; leave them for the release commit. After the release: `@DISTRO_ID@-update` on each installed system, and for a new install, the ISO in `build/iso/` written to a USB stick as in [Building the distro from source](#building-the-distro-from-source), step 7.

## Conventions

- One file, `distro.conf`, holds the distro's identity (name, identifier, base series, apt and GitHub settings). Never edit a rendered file; edit its `.in` and run `make render`.
- Every decision in `docs/` is a choice with reasons and the rejected alternative, dated, not a claimed best practice.
- Docs under `docs/` are dated records and are not templated; they name @DISTRO_NAME@ directly.
- Secrets never enter the repo: the signing key lives in GnuPG, logs with hardware details stay under `~/.local/state/@DISTRO_ID@/`.
- Nothing in this repo commits on your behalf; `make publish PUBLISH_FLAGS=--push`, `make iso-publish`, `make apt-infra`, `make iso-infra` and `make budget-infra` are the only commands that change anything outside this workstation: the first two upload to the apt repository's and the ISO downloads' S3 buckets, the next two create or update those buckets' CloudFormation stacks, and the last creates or updates the account's cost budget.

## Documents

- [docs/0 - plan.md](0%20-%20plan.md): the approved plan. Options compared, the Ubuntu base, NVIDIA, AppArmor, the Legion laptop's hardware, sleep, the roadmap and the verification list.
- [docs/1 - policy.md](1%20-%20policy.md): roadmap step 1. Name, base series and move triggers, Omarchy tag and merge policy, naming and version rules, the Secure Boot and encryption requirements, the password login rule with its fingerprint and FIDO2 key paths, and why hibernation is out of the first release.
- [docs/2 - build-infra.md](2%20-%20build-infra.md): roadmap step 2. The tools above with their rejected alternatives, one-time setup, key rotation, the rebrand playbook, and what was verified.
- [docs/3 - rootfs.md](3%20-%20rootfs.md): roadmap step 3. What the base metapackage pulls in and why, the files the build writes into the root filesystem, the choices (os-release diversion, netplan kept, Recommends on with two packages pinned out), how to run the build and the boot test, and what was verified
- [docs/4 - session.md](4%20-%20session.md): roadmap step 4. What the session metapackage pulls in, the NVIDIA install path (ubuntu-drivers, prebuilt signed modules, early KMS hook), SDDM's Wayland greeter under Hyprland, the choices, the graphical boot test, the driver check on this laptop, and what was verified.
- [docs/5 - hyprland-quickshell.md](5%20-%20hyprland-quickshell.md): roadmap step 5. Omarchy's Hyprland and Quickshell pins against the archive, why Hyprland stays and Quickshell is backported, how a third-party package is built from Debian's packaging plus an upstream tarball, and the QEMU check that runs Omarchy's shell.
- [docs/6 - omarchy-port.md](6%20-%20omarchy-port.md): roadmap step 6. The fork and its port branch, the rename script, how Omarchy's two packages become five, what the port changed (apt, PAM, Ubuntu names, drops), the third-party packages, the choices (no browser in the metapackage, no migrations, install-time setup as a script), the desktop boot test, and what was verified.
- [docs/7 - image-installer.md](7%20-%20image-installer.md): roadmap step 7. The live ISO (casper, the squashfs, the EFI partition image with Ubuntu's signed shim and GRUB), the installer (Calamares from Ubuntu's archive, its module sequence and every configuration), the Plymouth theme through alternatives, the boot test under OVMF with Secure Boot on and off, what the installed system still needs checked by hand, and the KPMcore rebuild that lets an encrypted install work in every partitioning mode (choice 11, 2026-10-11).
- [docs/8 - update-path.md](8%20-%20update-path.md): roadmap step 8. How an installed system updates (`@DISTRO_ID@-update` on apt, unattended security updates from Ubuntu), the migrations runner and the rule for the port's own migrations, the N to N+1 upgrade test and its CI job, the version-bump check and the publish guard, the unattended-upgrades origin bug and its fix, what the series upgrade will need, and what was verified.
- [docs/suspend-baseline.md](suspend-baseline.md): the three measured suspend scenarios on the Legion laptop, the known noise, and the phantom-backlight finding.
- the fork's [PORT-REMOVALS.md](@OMARCHY_FORK@/blob/@OMARCHY_PORT_REF@/PORT-REMOVALS.md): the removal audit. What the port still lacks from Omarchy @OMARCHY_TAG@, why, whether the reason holds on Ubuntu, and everything restored since 2026-10-16 with its choice.
